# Zhuyin (Bopomofo) → Hanyu Pinyin, by syllable. Pinyin cannot be read letter by
# letter from Zhuyin: the 1958 scheme (汉语拼音方案, approved by the National
# People's Congress on 1958-02-11) spells a syllable by rules, so the table lists
# whole syllables, each in its tones. Built from three sources:
#   - the scheme's initial and final tables, which print each one's Zhuyin, and its
#     spelling rules: zhi chi shi ri zi ci si (1), er (2), ê (3), the y and w forms
#     and ju qu xu (4), and iu ui un after an initial (5);
#   - the syllables and their tone-marked spellings: every kMandarin reading in
#     Unihan (UCD 18.0.0), so a row exists only for a syllable and tone some
#     character has, and the tone mark sits where Unicode's data puts it;
#   - the Zhuyin tone marks: 國語注音符號手冊 (Taiwan Ministry of Education, 2000),
#     調號說明表: tones 1-4 after the syllable's last symbol, tone 1 "may be omitted",
#     the neutral dot before the first symbol in horizontal writing; codepoints from
#     The Unicode Standard, Table 18-9 (ˉ U+02C9, ˊ U+02CA, ˇ U+02C7, ˋ U+02CB, ˙ U+02D9).
# A syllable with no mark is tone 1, so it reads as tone 1 where one exists. Every
# syllable has a neutral (˙) row, as any syllable can be neutral in a word. Left out:
# the apostrophe between syllables (rule 5, pi'ao) and word division, which need the
# word; erhua's r (rule 2), written as a following ㄦ; the shorthand ẑ ĉ ŝ ŋ; and 7
# kMandarin readings the scheme gives no spelling for: ň ǹ ǹg ḿ (syllabic nasals),
# yō, wòng, and the two-syllable túnwǎ.
